---
import DocsLayout from '@/layouts/DocsLayout.astro';
import { Callout } from '@/molecules/DocComponents/DocComponents';
---

<DocsLayout breadcrumbs={[{ label: 'Docs', href: '/docs' }, { label: 'Architecture', href: '/docs/architecture' }, { label: 'Agents', href: '/docs/architecture/agents' }, { label: 'LORE (Writer)' }]}>
  <h1>LORE  -  Writer Agent</h1>

  <p>
    LORE is the <strong>writer</strong>. It documents decisions and updates changelogs after a merge. LORE activates
    after work is merged and never interrupts active development. It is <strong>often disabled by default</strong> —
    you enable it when you want documentation kept up to date as the team delivers.
  </p>

  <p>
    LORE acts directly but its actions are bounded by its role: it only documents <em>merged</em> work. It records
    decisions and maintains the changelog and history so that the team's output is captured — without slowing
    down the build, test, review, and merge cycle.
  </p>

  <h2>What LORE does</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Documents merged work.</strong> LORE triggers after a successful merge and captures what changed and why.</li>
    <li><strong>Updates changelogs.</strong> It maintains the project's changelog and history as work lands.</li>
    <li><strong>Stays out of the way.</strong> LORE runs post-merge, so it never interrupts active development or blocks a merge.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>How it fits into the team</h2>
  <p>
    After VESSEL merges a pull request, LORE activates to document the completed work — the final step in the
    cycle before control returns to NEXUS to pick up the next ticket.
  </p>

  <Callout type="info" title="Enabled when you want it">
    Because LORE is a documentation pass rather than a delivery step, it's often off by default. When you turn it
    on, it keeps history current without changing how the rest of the pipeline works.
  </Callout>
</DocsLayout>
